<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dongzhi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dongzhi Festival, Tōji, Dongji, Tunji, Đông chí</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dongzhi is the 22nd solar term of the Chinese calendar, marking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>, and the festival that Chinese people observe at that time, around 21 to 23 December. Its origins are traced to the philosophy of yin and yang, the lengthening days representing positive energy. The festival was first celebrated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> (1045 BCE–256 BCE) and declared an official celebration during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> (206 BCE–220 CE), when people took a break from work, worshipped heaven and honoured their ancestors. Families still gather to eat tangyuan, balls of glutinous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> that symbolize reunion, and dumplings; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, where the day is called Dongji, porridge is made with red beans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dongzhi.jpg" alt="Dongzhi" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alpha from Melbourne, Australia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:汤圆_Cooked_Tang_Yuan_(329781165).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
